]]><![CDATA[<p>More crackle paste! But now it&rsquo;s backlit. Here is what it looks like in daylight.</p>


 





























<a href="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_front_hu12098327063131591131.jpg">
    <img class="md-image"
         src="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_front_hu12098327063131591131.jpg"
         alt=""
         loading="lazy">
</a>


<p>The initial version consisted of the canvas and an LED strip glued to cardboard. I figured this was not the biggest brain move, so I instead got some fancy fireproof material from the local hardware store and glued the LEDs to that.</p>


 





























<a href="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_backside_hu13125094772923760222.jpg">
    <img class="md-image"
         src="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_backside_hu13125094772923760222.jpg"
         alt=""
         loading="lazy">
</a>


<p>I wanted to be able to remove the lights from the canvas, so I used adhesive velcro strips to attach the fancy material to the frame.</p>


 





























<a href="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_separated_hu17215606027426920904.jpg">
    <img class="md-image"
         src="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_separated_hu17215606027426920904.jpg"
         alt=""
         loading="lazy">
</a>


<p>I put some black insulation tape to the sides in order to block the light from coming out.</p>


 





























<a href="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_tape_hu10137989637990841126.jpg">
    <img class="md-image"
         src="https://tlocrt.com/other/art/cracks/images/cracks_tape_hu10137989637990841126.jpg"
         alt=""
         loading="lazy">
</a>


